<h2>Links in emails: callback tokens</h2>
<p>Most links in emails don't point straight at a page. They point to <code>/ValidateToken?token=…</code>, where the token is a small JSON object <code>{ email, ExpirationTime, RedirectUrl }</code> encrypted and signed with ASP.NET Core Data Protection (purpose string <code>"User  Registration"</code>, two spaces). The page decrypts it, checks the expiry, and redirects to <code>RedirectUrl</code>.</p>
<div class="docs-table-wrap">
    <table class="docs-table">
        <thead><tr><th>Email</th><th>Token lifetime</th><th>Redirects to</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr><td>Invitation</td><td>1 day</td><td>The register page for that email.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Event created / deleted / "Do you still need this?"</td><td>1 day</td><td>The church's events page.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Church details sent to a visitor</td><td>1 day</td><td><code>/Contact?cid=…</code> for that church.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Password reset</td><td>2 hours (and the Identity reset code itself expires after 2 hours)</td><td>The reset password page.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
</div>
<p>Because the tokens are protected by the site's Data Protection keys, they stop working if the key ring is lost or replaced. The ring is shared through blob storage, so all instances and restarts can read each other's tokens.</p>

<h2>Protected IDs in pages and forms</h2>
<p>Internal IDs (user IDs, church IDs, event IDs, role names, emails in admin forms) are never put in pages or URLs as plain text. The <code>Encoder</code> class protects them with named Data Protection protectors, so a value from one page can't be replayed on another:</p>
<p><code>fc.data.contact</code>, <code>fc.data.user.events</code>, <code>fc.data.admin.events</code>, <code>fc.data.url.email</code>, <code>fc.data.url.change</code>, <code>fc.data.manage.church</code>, <code>fc.data.manage.email</code>, <code>fc.data.mail.confirm</code>, <code>fc.data.login.external</code>, <code>fc.data.admin.church</code>, <code>fc.data.admin.contact</code>, <code>fc.data.admin.invite</code>, <code>fc.data.admin.role</code>, <code>fc.data.admin.users</code>, <code>fc.data.church.information</code>, <code>fc.data.google</code>.</p>
<p>An "invalid or expired link" message in Site admin usually means a page was left open across a key rotation (keys last 7 days) or a deploy that lost the keys; reopen the page from the menu.</p>

<h2>Sign-in</h2>
<ol class="docs-steps">
    <li>The login form looks the user up <strong>by email</strong>, then checks the password with <code>CheckPasswordSignInAsync</code> (falling back to <code>PasswordSignInAsync</code> by username).</li>
    <li>If the email isn't confirmed: "Sign-in is not allowed for this account because the email address has not been confirmed."</li>
    <li>If two-factor is on, the user is sent to <code>LoginWith2fa</code> (6-digit TOTP code) or <code>LoginWithRecoveryCode</code>. <em>Remember this device</em> sets a two-factor remember cookie.</li>
    <li>On success an Identity cookie is issued.</li>
</ol>
<div class="docs-table-wrap">
    <table class="docs-table">
        <thead><tr><th>Setting</th><th>Value</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr><td>Password rules</td><td>At least 12 characters; upper case, lower case, digit and symbol required.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Account lockout</td><td><strong>Off</strong> (<code>lockoutOnFailure: false</code>): unlimited password attempts.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Auth cookie</td><td><code>Secure</code>, <code>HttpOnly</code>, <code>SameSite=None</code>; default 14-day lifetime with <em>Keep me signed in</em>, session cookie without; sliding expiration off.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Role/permission changes</td><td>Permission checks read the user's roles from the database on every request, so they apply immediately. Checks by role name (SuperAdmin-only actions, which menus are shown) use the roles in the cookie, refreshed at the next sign-in or security-stamp revalidation (every 30 minutes by default).</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Email confirmation and password reset tokens</td><td>2 hours.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Session (server-side state, used by ChurchSuite PKCE)</td><td>30-minute idle timeout, held in memory on the instance.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Antiforgery</td><td>Cookie <code>X-CSRF-TOKEN-COOKIE</code>, header <code>X-CSRF-TOKEN-HEADER</code>; checked on every POST/PUT/DELETE except login, register, logout, external login and <code>/api</code>.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
</div>

<h2>How permissions are checked</h2>
<p>Each permission is registered as an authorisation policy of the same name (<code>PolicyConfiguration.AddPolicies</code>). A page marked <code>[Authorize(Policy = "UserManagement.Read")]</code> is handled by <code>PermissionAuthorizationHandler</code>, which loads the signed-in user's current roles from the database and checks the <code>RolePermissions</code> table for that permission. Both changing a role's permissions and changing a user's roles therefore take effect on the next request. A few checks use the role <em>name</em> from the sign-in cookie instead (<code>[Authorize(Roles = "SuperAdmin")]</code> on Emails, <code>IsInRole("SuperAdmin")</code> for editing and deleting users, ELMAH, and whether the Admin menu link and admin guide are shown); those follow the cookie.</p>
